# Fathom — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Fathom carries a small debugging and automation surface, `window.__fathom`, so the
dive can be driven and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or
waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build installs the finished surface on `window.__fathom` as soon as the game has
initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is
present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read or a pose of the game's state. A pose sets one thing, and
the game's own sensing, pathfinding, release schedule and contact rules run from
there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario driven from code behaves exactly
like one played by hand.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Fathom carries a small debugging and automation surface so the dive can be driven
and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as
the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from `engine.debug`,
and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the page. Every scenario
driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is present and exactly as
specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `FathomState`, written in the shape of
`update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a writable
one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<FathomState>`, and returns
the next `FathomState`; a reading takes the state the same way and returns what it
read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.setScreen(s, "playing"))`, and a reading against the
engine's current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`. A pose sets one thing,
and the game's own sensing, pathfinding, release schedule and contact rules run
from there exactly as they do in play, so a scenario driven from code behaves
exactly like one played by hand. `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type,
`FathomDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{else}}
Fathom carries a small debugging and automation surface so the dive can be driven
and inspected from code without touching the keyboard or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
game instance's `initialize` returns the finished surface. The engine holds it and
returns it from `engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is
installed on the page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through
it, so it is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation acts on the live game at the moment it is called. A pose takes only
the parameters its heading names, arranges the running game through the same
systems play uses, and returns nothing; a reading takes no parameters, returns
plain data built at the call, and changes nothing. A pose sets one thing, and the
game's own sensing, pathfinding, release schedule and contact rules run from there
exactly as they do in play, so a scenario driven from code behaves exactly like
one played by hand. Fathom runs in one world for the whole session and every
screen is a value of `screen`, so a pose that changes the screen takes effect at
the call rather than riding a level transition. `src/game.ts` declares and exports
its type, `FathomDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls it.
{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the fixed-step core `specs/movement.md` fixes: the
simulation advances in whole `TICK_DT` ticks at `TICK_HZ` (`120`) of them a
second, so a tick is the unit the operations below count in. Two more properties
make the game driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer.
- Seeded randomness. Every draw the game makes, for predator and drifter wander,
  for the bonus-drifter cadence, and for the maze it lays out, runs off a
  generator the surface's `reset` seeds, and the game keeps that generator's whole
  state, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result
  exactly.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the game
reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and the
surface carries no operation for either. That layer delivers the key bindings this
specification fixes, so a dispatched keyboard event drives the forager, the sonar
pulse, the ink, the menus, the pause and the mute exactly as a player's key does,
and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the game
frame by frame and a scripted clock takes it off real time, it drives the actions
the game registers so a dispatched keyboard event moves the forager exactly as a
player's key does, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Fathom's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`FATHOM_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings and booleans. Positions
and radii are in the logical units `specs/overview.md` fixes, `tx` and `ty` are
the column and row of the tile grid defined there, and every duration is in
seconds.

Each pose sets one thing and leaves the rest of the game as it stands, so a
caller that wants several things arranged makes several calls, in the order it
wants them. Nothing a caller does not ask for happens.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state, `DeepReadonly<FathomState>`.
A pose returns the next `FathomState`, built from the one it was handed, which it
leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The headings below name the
parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

An argument outside the domain its operation states is invalid, and the call fails
loudly rather than guessing what was meant. So is a call whose subject is not in
the condition the operation states, such as a predator index the roster does not
hold.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes only
when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself, which is
how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent tick left.

### `advance(ticks)`

Runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks immediately and in order, each worth exactly
one `TICK_DT`. The unit is ticks rather than seconds, so nothing is rounded: `advance(1)`
runs one tick and `advance(120)` covers one second of game time. Each tick is a real
tick, the same one the frame loop runs, so the game's own sensing, pathfinding and
contact rules produce the result, and the canvas is redrawn to reflect it.

`ticks` is a non-negative whole number, and `advance(0)` runs nothing.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a reproducible scenario calls `setAutoStep(false)`
first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every field of the observable state defined in `specs/state.md` to its
title-screen value: the title screen with its first menu item highlighted, the
score at `0`, `START_LIVES` (`3`) lives, depth `1`, brightness `0` with its hold
spent, a freshly laid out maze with a plankton on every corridor tile, the fog
fully unrevealed, no drifters, the roster depth `1` gives with every predator on a
den tile and unreleased, every creature's mind and travel running, both cooldowns
ready, no pulses and no ink clouds in flight, and `simTime` at `0`.
`options.seed` seeds the game's random generator, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED`
(`1`).
`options` is optional and an omitted `seed` takes the default.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A reset also re-arms manual stepping, leaving the game off the wall clock.
{{/if}}

`muted` is untouched, because muting is a player preference rather than a value a
dive opens with.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object `specs/state.md` defines.
It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `setScreen(state, s)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the game, returned as the plain object `specs/state.md` defines. It
changes nothing.

### `setScreen(s)`
{{/if}}

Sets `screen` to `s`, one of the seven screens `specs/state.md` names, and changes
no other field. The game carries on under its own rules from there, so each
screen's own behavior follows: the dive countdown counts down on `"countdown"`,
the maze is frozen on `"paused"`, and the staggered release schedule
`specs/predators.md` fixes takes its origin from the moment `screen` becomes
`"playing"`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setScore(state, points)`
{{else}}
### `setScore(points)`
{{/if}}

Sets the running score to the whole number `points`, at least `0`. Play carries on
from that figure, so the next plankton eaten adds `SCORE_PLANKTON` to it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setLives(state, n)`
{{else}}
### `setLives(n)`
{{/if}}

Sets the lives held in reserve to the whole number `n`, at least `0`. The life
being played is not among them, so `setLives(0)` leaves one attempt running and
the next contact ends the dive.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setDepth(state, d)`
{{else}}
### `setDepth(d)`
{{/if}}

Sets the current depth to the whole number `d`, at least `1`. What the
specification derives from depth follows: `sonar.range` becomes the `E`
`specs/progression.md` gives for depth `d`, and the roster becomes the one
`specs/predators.md` gives for depth `d`, laid out in the den with every
`released` flag false, exactly as a maze at that depth lays it out. It changes
neither the maze, the plankton, the fog nor the screen.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setMaze(state, rows)`
{{else}}
### `setMaze(rows)`
{{/if}}

Replaces the maze layout with the one given. `rows` is an array of `GRID_ROWS`
(`18`) strings of `GRID_COLS` (`36`) characters each, in exactly the alphabet the
snapshot's `tiles` uses: `'#'` rock, `'.'` corridor, `'g'` the den gate, and `'d'`
den interior. A layout of the wrong size, carrying any other character, or
carrying `'d'` or `'g'` tiles without exactly one `'g'`, is invalid.

The posed layout is a fixture and is used exactly as given. It is **exempt** from
every rule in `specs/maze.md`: dead ends, corridors wider than one tile,
asymmetry, disconnected regions, a missing wrap tunnel and a missing den are all
legal in a fixture, and the game keeps running on one. A structure the layout does
not have simply takes no part: with no den gate the bonus-drifter cadence has
nowhere to admit a drifter from and admits none, and on a layout with no den
chamber a predator whose `state` is `"den"` is held out of play, undrawn,
unmoving, and unable to make contact. The game accepts the fixture as it stands,
without repairing it, refusing it, stalling on it, or returning to a maze of its
own.

After the call the game behaves in every respect as though the posed layout were
the maze it had laid out itself. The wrap tunnel of a posed layout is the first
row, if any, whose column `0` and column `35` tiles are both open.

The layout is the whole of what it sets. The plankton, the revealed-tile memory,
the roster, every body's tile and facing, the cooldowns, the score, the lives, the
depth and the screen are all left exactly as they stand, so a caller poses each of
those itself. A body the new layout leaves on a tile closed to it holds that tile
and travels nowhere, because travel carries a body only along tiles open to it.
The fixture holds until the next `reset`, or until a descent lays out the next
maze.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPlankton(state, tx, ty, present)`
{{else}}
### `setPlankton(tx, ty, present)`
{{/if}}

Puts a plankton on tile `(tx, ty)` when `present` is `true` and takes one off when
it is `false`, and adjusts `planktonRemaining` to match. The tile is an open
corridor tile. Removing a plankton this way is not eating it, so it scores nothing
and clears no maze.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearPlankton(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearPlankton()`
{{/if}}

Takes every plankton off the maze at once, leaving `planktonRemaining` at `0`. As
with `setPlankton`, nothing here is eaten, so it scores nothing and clears no
maze: the maze is cleared by the forager eating a plankton when none remain after
it, and an empty maze the forager has not just eaten from stays in live play. With
no plankton in the maze the bonus-drifter cadence admits none, as
`specs/gameplay.md` fixes.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearFog(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearFog()`
{{/if}}

Puts every tile of the maze back to unrevealed, so `visibility` reports `u`
everywhere until light, sonar or a flare reaches a tile again. It moves nothing
and reveals nothing.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setForagerTile(state, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `setForagerTile(tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Moves the forager to the center of tile `(tx, ty)` and leaves it at rest there, as
though no movement key were held. The tile is an open corridor tile. Its facing is
untouched, and injected input moves it from there through the game's ordinary
movement code.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setForagerDir(state, dir)`
{{else}}
### `setForagerDir(dir)`
{{/if}}

Sets the forager's facing to `dir`, one of `"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"` and
`"right"`. It moves the forager nowhere and leaves it at rest.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setBrightness(state, g)`
{{else}}
### `setBrightness(g)`
{{/if}}

Sets the forager's brightness `G` to `g`, in `[0, 1]`. Everything derived from
brightness recomputes from it.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}
The light radius `V`, the vision-circle radius `R`, and the Lanternjaw's and
Flarefish's light detection ranges are those `specs/sensing.md`,
`specs/predators/lanternjaw.md` and `specs/predators/flarefish.md` give for `g`.
{{else}}
The light radius `V` and the Lanternjaw's and Flarefish's light detection ranges
are those `specs/sensing.md`, `specs/predators/lanternjaw.md` and
`specs/predators/flarefish.md` give for `g`.
{{/if}}
The brightness hold is left as it stands, so `G` decays from the posed value on
the ordinary curve as soon as whatever hold was running expires.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setBrightHold(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setBrightHold(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the seconds remaining on the brightness hold, from `0` to `BRIGHT_HOLD`
(`1.0 s`). While the hold runs `G` is steady, and at `0` it decays on the ordinary
curve. It changes `G` itself not at all, so posing a steady brightness is
`setBrightness` followed by `setBrightHold(BRIGHT_HOLD)`, which is the pair eating
a plankton arms.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearPredators(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearPredators()`
{{/if}}

Takes every predator off the board at once, leaving the roster empty and
`predators` an empty list. The removal catches nobody and scores nothing, and no
release schedule runs, because there is no predator left to run one. The roster
stays empty until a call adds one, or until `reset`, `setDepth`, or a maze laid
out by a descent or a lost life gives the depth its own roster again.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `addPredator(state, kind, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `addPredator(kind, tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Adds one predator of `kind`, one of `"lanternjaw"`, `"gloamfin"` and
`"flarefish"`, at the center of tile `(tx, ty)`, an open corridor tile. It is
added at the end of `predators`, so its index is the number of predators the
roster held before the call, and it is added whatever roster the current depth
gives, so a posed roster may hold any kinds in any number.

It arrives loose and patrolling: its `state` is `"wander"`, its `released` flag is
`true`, its mind and its travel are running, and it faces `"up"`. It hunts,
senses, chases and makes contact from there exactly as a predator released from
the den does. It carries no release time, because the staggered schedule
`specs/predators.md` fixes runs on the roster a maze is laid out with.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorTile(state, index, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorTile(index, tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Moves one predator to the center of tile `(tx, ty)` and leaves it there. `index`
selects a predator by its position in the snapshot's `predators` list, counted
from `0`. The tile is an open corridor tile, a den tile or the den gate. Its
facing, its `state`, its `released` flag, its mind and its travel are untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorDir(state, index, dir)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorDir(index, dir)`
{{/if}}

Sets one predator's facing to `dir`, one of `"up"`, `"down"`, `"left"` and
`"right"`. `index` selects the predator as above.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorState(state, index, value)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorState(index, value)`
{{/if}}

Sets one predator's `state` to `value`, one of `"den"`, `"wander"` and `"chase"`.
`index` selects the predator as above. `"search"` is reached only through the
Gloamfin's own behavior and is not posed. It moves the predator nowhere and leaves
its `released` flag as it stands. After the call the predator's own mind runs
whenever the simulation advances: it senses, acquires, chases and searches through
the real code.

| `value` | What it poses | Valid while the predator stands on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `"den"` | The predator is in the den chamber, holding a fix on nothing and drawn nowhere. | A den tile or the den gate. |
| `"wander"` | The predator is loose on the tile it stands on, patrolling, with no fix on the forager. | An open corridor tile. |
| `"chase"` | The predator is loose on the tile it stands on, with a fix on the forager's current tile, pursuing it. | An open corridor tile. |

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorReleased(state, index, released)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorReleased(index, released)`
{{/if}}

Sets one predator's `released` flag, which says whether its turn in the staggered
schedule has come. `index` selects the predator as above. It moves the predator
nowhere and changes its `state` not at all.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorMind(state, index, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorMind(index, enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns one predator's own mind on or off. `index` selects the predator as above.
Minds are on by default and a `reset` restores them.

With its mind off, that predator senses nothing and decides nothing, keeping the
facing, the `state` and the fix it was posed with. Nothing is decided, so nothing
is carried out and it holds exactly where it stands, however long the scenario
runs. It is still drawn under the rule its kind and its lighting give, and contact
with it still costs a life. Every other predator, and everything else in the game,
carries on untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setPredatorTravel(state, index, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setPredatorTravel(index, enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns one predator's travel on or off. `index` selects the predator as above.
Travel is on by default and a `reset` restores it.

With its travel off, that predator's body holds the tile it stands on, however
long the scenario runs and whatever its mind decides. Its mind runs untouched: it
senses, takes and lapses a fix, fires its detection alert, changes its `state`
under its own rules, and reports the `speed` that `state` carries. Its `released`
flag still turns over when its slot in the release schedule arrives, and it holds
in the den from there, because crossing the chamber to the gate is travel. It is
still drawn under the rule its kind and its lighting give, and contact with it
still costs a life. Every other predator, and everything else in the game, carries
on untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `spawnDrifter(state, tx, ty)`
{{else}}
### `spawnDrifter(tx, ty)`
{{/if}}

Adds one bonus drifter at the center of tile `(tx, ty)`, an open corridor tile. It
is added at the end of `drifters`, so its index is the number of drifters the maze
held before the call. It then wanders through the ordinary drifter code and is
worth the ordinary bonus when eaten. It is added whatever the drifter cadence is
doing and whatever the `DRIFTER_MAX` (`2`) ceiling would otherwise allow.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `clearDrifters(state)`
{{else}}
### `clearDrifters()`
{{/if}}

Takes every bonus drifter off the maze at once, leaving `drifters` an empty list.
None of them is eaten, so nothing is scored. The cadence carries on from there and
tops the maze back up on its own schedule while plankton remain.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setDrifterMind(state, index, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setDrifterMind(index, enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns one drifter's own mind on or off. `index` selects a drifter by its position
in the snapshot's `drifters` list, counted from `0`. Minds are on by default and a
`reset` restores them.

With its mind off, that drifter decides nothing, so nothing is carried out and it
holds exactly where it stands, however long the scenario runs. It is still drawn,
still eaten by a forager whose tile it shares, and still worth the ordinary bonus.
Every other drifter, and everything else in the game, carries on untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setDrifterTravel(state, index, enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setDrifterTravel(index, enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns one drifter's travel on or off. `index` selects the drifter as above. Travel
is on by default and a `reset` restores it.

With its travel off, that drifter's body holds the tile it stands on, however long
the scenario runs and whatever its wander decides. It is still drawn, still eaten
by a forager whose tile it shares, and still worth the ordinary bonus. Every other
drifter, and everything else in the game, carries on untouched.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setSonarCooldown(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setSonarCooldown(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the seconds remaining on the sonar pulse's cooldown, at least `0`. At `0` the
pulse is ready and the snapshot reports `sonar.ready` as `true`. The cooldown runs
down from the posed value on the ordinary curve.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `setInkCooldown(state, seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setInkCooldown(seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the seconds remaining on ink's cooldown, at least `0`. At `0` ink is ready and
the snapshot reports `ink.ready` as `true`. The cooldown runs down from the posed
value on the ordinary curve.

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only. It shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources, it draws over the running game without changing anything, and
it is hidden until it is toggled. Register at least the current `screen` and
`depth`, the `score` and `lives`, the brightness `G`, the light radius `V`,
{{#if (eq variant.slug "kindle")}}the vision-circle radius `R`, {{/if}}the sonar and ink cooldowns, the plankton
remaining, the forager's tile and facing, and, for each predator, its kind,
state, tile, speed and whether its alert is firing. Those are the same facts the
snapshot reports. Keep each source short enough to read on a line and keep every
one a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the simulation as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources in the game's monospace type, plainly and clearly apart from the HUD, and
the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`) shows and hides it. It starts
hidden.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Fathom's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read, as
`DeepReadonly<FathomState>`, and reports the value that state carries. Drawing the
panel, toggling it with the backtick key, and keeping it read-only are the
engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Fathom's part, through the diagnostics
registries the engine provides. Each source is a function of no arguments, called
at each read, reporting the live game at the moment it runs. Drawing the panel,
toggling it with the backtick key, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
